# Ticket #004 – Switch Connection

**Status:** Resolved  
**Date:** 2026-07-31  
**Category:** Networking / Office / Switch / Ethernet  
**Environment:** Cisco  WRVS4400N Wireless-N Gigabit Security Router with VPN, PC running on Windows 11 Pro 

---

## Issue
Training room is on a separate floor from the server room & has very weak wifi signal. Only a single ethernet port connected to the main router is available in the entire training room.

## Troubleshooting Steps
- Converted a decommissioned "Cisco WRVS4400N Wireless-N Gigabit Security Router with VPN" to serve as an access point (AP) by the following steps:
1. Directly connected router to pc using rj45 ethernet cable with one end in the pc & the other end into the router's WAN port.
2. Confirmed successful connection & router details using the "ipconfig" command in the pc's cmd.
3. Noted that this particular router does not have a default access point operation mode.
4. Recorded all router's details printed from the ipconfig results.
5. Copied the router's ethernet default gateway from the ipconfig result.
6. Pasted the copied gateway ip address in the browser's address bar to access the router's management & admin's settings.
7. Entered admin-admin respectively as username-password credentials in order to log into the router's management settings.
8. Entered the LAN settings inside the router's management.
9. In the LAN setting, I changed the router's local ip address from 192.168.1.1 to 192.168.1.3.  
10. Recorded the new router's local ip address for future management login access.  
11. Still inside the LAN settings, I disabled the dhcp.
12. Clicked on save, then waited for the router to reboot. After reboot, router would serve as an access point.
13. Disconnected ethernet cable from router's WAN port & reconnected into the router's LAN port 2 instead.
14. Connected router to the wall ethernet port to connect it to the main router using another ethernet cable with one end of the cable into the wall port, & the other end into the router's LAN port 1. 
13. Navigated to Windows ethernet settings through *'Win+i >  Network & internet > Ethernet > edit IP assignment'*.
14. Changed IP settings from "Automatic (DHCP)" to "manual", then toggled on "IPv4".
15. Set IP address field to 192.168.1.50 (changed only the network id of the ethernet default gateway gotten from the ipconfig command to a unique & valid number i.e. the last number in the ip address).
16. Set Subnet mask to 255.255.255.0
17. Set Gateway to 192.168.1.3 (changed only the network id of the ethernet default gateway gotten from the ipconfig command to another unique & valid number i.e. the last number in the ip address).
18. Set Preferred DNS to 8.8.8.8 (free & public DNS server by Google).
19. Save settings.
20. 

## Root Cause
Insufficient adjacent ethernet sockets (rj45 female sockets) to serve clients.

## Resolution
The issue was resolved by implementing a simple desktop switch to the network connection in order toexpand the LAN capacity;  
1. Connected the switch to the main router via one of the two available wall sockets using an ethernet cable with rj45 connectors.  
2. Connected the access point router to the main router via the second wall socket using an ethernet cable with rj45 connectors.  
3. Separately connected both user pc's to the switch in order to access the main router, using ethernet cables with rj45 connectors.  

## Verification
- Users successfully accessed the internet using wifi connection from the access point router.
- Users successfully accessed the main server through the main router via the LAN connections on the switch.  

## Status
Resolved. End-users confirmed operational.

## Notes
- For routers that do not have default access point (AP) modes (where you have to switch to AP by manually setting router's ip *[default gateway ip]* & disabling dchp), connect ethernet cable into the router's ethernet LAN 1 port & not the WAN port, for connection to the main router or switch.
- For routers that have default access point (AP) modes, connect ethernet cable into the router's ethernet WAN port, for connection to the main router or switch.


## Lessons Learned
- Familiarize with the type of router that is being used as access point, & take the notes above into consideration.

